<!--
Savannah: an acacia, flat-crowned, on a low horizon.

  Three versions of this were a beach umbrella and the reasons are worth
  keeping, because each fix on its own was not enough:

    * a smooth closed curve over a stem is an umbrella however flat you draw
      it — the crown here is a ragged mass, and a lumpy silhouette cannot be a
      manufactured object;
    * the horizontal ground line under it was the umbrella's base, so the
      horizon does the grounding instead;
    * a centre branch running up the middle was the pole. Two side branches
      fork below the crown, where an acacia's actually do, and nothing runs up
      the axis.

  The sun is low, right, and cut by the horizon. A whole disc in the sky read as
  a balloon, and on the left it read as a hill sitting where the branches are.

  Drawn in white and tinted at the call site with the zone's own ZoneAccent.
-->
